<title>AUSTRALIA: Australia stocks seen tentatively firmer on Dow.</title>
<headline>Australia stocks seen tentatively firmer on Dow.</headline>
<text>
<p>------------------(Snapshot 8.35 a.m./2235 GMT)----------------</p>
<p>AORD		 2563.9   +16.0   DJIA	   7345.91   +87.78</p>
<p>SPI Sycom	  2565.0    +7.0   US 30yr	   6.99    steady</p>
<p>10yrbond futs    92.370  -0.050   Gold NY 344.40 Lon fix 341.65</p>
<p>AUD  NY $0.7670 (local $0.7663)   CRB Index    251.31    -0.62</p>
<p>NIKKEI	   20009.00 +131.61   FTSE	    4661.8   +10.0</p>
<p>S&amp;P500(Globex)   847.75   +7.50   NZSE40 open  2311.92    +8.23</p>
<p>--------------------------(May 26)-----------------------------</p>
<p>*Stocks expected to be up on Dow, but seen prone to profit taking. &quot;I can't see it racing ahead. The move in the Dow sounds good, but how real is it?&quot; said Nevitts broker Jim Trendenick.  </p>
<p>*Pre-open bids A$19.33 (+0.01), A$18.75 (-0.04), A$5.76 (+0.02), A$8.28 (unch)</p>
<p>*Dow jumps 88 points to new high on pre-Memorial day buying. High tech and mid-cap stocks strengthen with blue chips.</p>
<p>*ADRs: News Corp 17 7/8 (-1/8), BHP 29 3/4 (-1/8), WMC 25 5/8 (-1/8), National Australia Bank 72 3/8 (+3/8)</p>
<p>*Copper closes down US$6 a tonne at US$2,520. Aluminium unchanged, nickel slumps US$110 a tonne to $7,530.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
